    <body>
      <sec id="article-31453.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica</italic>, a gram-negative bacillus transmitted through contaminated food, untreated water, household animals, and transfused blood products, causes yersiniosis with presentations ranging from acute diarrhea to mesenteric adenitis, terminal ileitis, and pseudoappendicitis. This course reviews the organism's pathogenesis, including its localization in lymphoid tissue and the role of virulence factors such as pYV, urease, Ail, YadA, and Yops. Postinfectious sequelae, including reactive arthritis and erythema nodosum, are also discussed. This course also addresses the evaluation and management of this pathogen, emphasizing targeted stool testing due to prolonged fecal shedding after symptom resolution, supportive care&#x000a0;as the foundation of management, and, in selected patients, the use of antimicrobial therapy and surgical evaluation when appendicitis cannot be confidently excluded. Potential complications involve both gastrointestinal and extraintestinal systems, including sepsis, abscess formation, and myocarditis.</p>
        <p>This activity&#x000a0;reviews the transmission patterns, virulence mechanisms, diagnostic strategies, and management considerations for yersiniosis. Participants gain insight into&#x000a0;conditions that mimic yersiniosis, such as pseudoappendicitis, diagnostic stool and imaging findings, potential complications, and evidence-based decisions regarding antimicrobial therapy, hospitalization, infection-control precautions, and preventive counseling.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying <italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic> infections, conducting the recommended evaluation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach to manage this condition, thereby improving patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica</italic>.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based diagnostic strategies to confirm an infection with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica</italic>.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate indications for each management approach&#x000a0;in patients with&#x000a0;clinical features of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica</italic>&#x000a0;infection.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement interprofessional team strategies to improve care coordination for patients with <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica&#x000a0;</italic>infection.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=31453&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=31453">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica</italic> is a gram-negative bacillus-shaped bacterium that causes a zoonotic disease called yersiniosis (see <bold>Image.</bold> Gram-Stained Appearance of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica</italic>. The&#x000a0;infection&#x000a0;is manifested as acute diarrhea, mesenteric adenitis, terminal ileitis, and pseudoappendicitis. In rare cases, it can even cause sepsis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>In some countries, <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> infections have overtaken <italic toggle="yes">Shigella</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Salmonella</italic> species as the most common cause of bacterial gastroenteritis. While most cases are sporadic, large outbreaks are not uncommon.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r3">[3]</xref> Humans acquire <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> after consumption of contaminated food, as well as a blood transfusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r1">[1]</xref> The key feature of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> is that the individual continues to shed the organism in feces for nearly 3 months after symptoms have subsided; thus, detecting&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> in stool samples is critical.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The genus <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia&#x000a0;</italic>includes&#x000a0;26 species, 3 of which are known to cause diseases in humans&#x02014;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic>,<italic toggle="yes"> Y pestis</italic>, and<italic toggle="yes"> Y pseudotuberculosis</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>Yersinioses are zoonotic infections in which humans serve as incidental hosts,&#x000a0;not contributing to the pathogen's life cycle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica&#x000a0;</italic>have been isolated from a variety of animals, with pigs being the most common source. The pathogen&#x000a0;can spread from one pig to another in a herd. The&#x000a0;bacteria can contaminate pork products, including neck trimmings, tongue, and tonsils, and can spread to other meat cuts during slaughter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Yersiniae</italic> are classified based on their biochemical features. Of the 6 biotypes, subtypes 3 and 4 cause more than 80% of human cases of yersiniosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Worldwide, <italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic> is estimated to cause 2% of cases of gastroenteritis. The pooled prevalence of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica&#x02013;</italic>associated gastroenteritis is higher in low-income than in high-income countries, with no observed differences by age group or gender.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;In the United States, yersinosis incidence rates among Asian and African American children are approximately 2 to 3 times higher than&#x000a0;in White children; however, no differences in incidence by race have been observed in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;In addition,&#x000a0;the annual crude incidence of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic> among African American children younger than&#x000a0;5&#x000a0;markedly declined in the first decade of the 21st century.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia </italic>species are classified based on&#x000a0;phenotype and serotype. Phenotypically, these&#x000a0;bacteria are divided into 6 biotypes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r8">[8]</xref> Serologically, this pathogen is classified into more than&#x000a0;70 O serotypes;&#x000a0;however, only a few of these are pathogenic. The pathogenic serotypes are O:3 (biogroup 4), O:5,27 (biogroup 2 and 3), O:8 (biogroup 1B), and O:9 (biogroup 2). The most common serotype isolated from humans in European countries is O:3, followed by O:9.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r8">[8]</xref> In the United States, serogroup O:8 is the most prevalent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Infection is transferred predominantly through the fecal-oral route. Pork consumption, especially of undercooked or raw pork products, is responsible for yersiniosis.&#x000a0;Outbreaks have also been reported in Norway and New Zealand from untreated drinking water contaminated with this pathogen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;There are case reports of infection being transferred from an infected household pet and through transfused blood products.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>The pathogen&#x000a0;enters the stomach in food, traverses the gut wall, and spreads to lymphoid tissue (Peyer's patches) and mesenteric lymph nodes.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia&#x000a0;</italic>bacteria contain a 70-kDa virulence plasmid, pYV, and produce ureases that metabolize urea into ammonia, which protects the bacteria from the stomach's harsh acidic environment. The bacteria&#x000a0;contain the gene&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Ail</italic> (attachment invasion locus) and express the cell-surface adhesin YadA, both of which facilitate attachment to host cells. <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> outer proteins (Yops) perform many functions that promote infection, including arresting phagocytosis&#x000a0;and blocking secretion of mediators such as tumor necrosis factor-alpha and interleukin-8.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Pathogenic <italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica&#x000a0;</italic>strains secrete the enterotoxin&#x000a0;YstI, which induces diarrhea by increasing cyclic guanosine monophosphate in intestinal epithelial cells and in the extracellular fluid of the intestinal lumen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> infection can cause&#x000a0;extraintestinal complications, including reactive arthritis, erythema nodosum, and conjunctivitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;The pathogenesis of reactive arthritis is likely due to&#x000a0;a combination of HLA-B27 antigen positivity and vigorous anti-<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> immunoglobulin (Ig) A and IgG immune responses in the synovial fluid.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>One unique feature of <italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic> is that it cannot chelate iron, and its growth is limited in low-iron environments. Individuals with conditions that cause iron overload, such as hemochromatosis, thalassemia, or disorders of iron metabolism, have a higher risk of severe&#x000a0;yersiniosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> infections are often asymptomatic; however,&#x000a0;when symptomatic, they typically cause acute infections that begin in the intestines and spread to other organs.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic>&#x000a0;manifestations can be broadly classified as gastrointestinal and extraintestinal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Gastrointestinal Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Yersiniosis&#x000a0;is typically self-limiting.&#x000a0;Symptoms commonly begin with fever and&#x000a0;abdominal pain, and progress to nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea, which can be bloody.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Yersiniosis is&#x000a0;often overlooked in cases of acute diarrhea because of its similarity to other causes of infectious diarrhea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Acute yersiniosis&#x000a0;may cause mesenteric lymphadenitis with&#x000a0;right lower quadrant abdominal pain&#x000a0;that may mimic appendicitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Magnetic resonance imaging may demonstrate terminal ileal inflammation with a normal appendix.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r19">[19]</xref> Pseudoappendicitis is most common in older children and young adults and may result in unnecessary surgical interventions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Prolonged fecal&#x000a0;shedding commonly occurs after symptom resolution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Extraintestinal Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Common extraintestinal manifestations of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> infection include reactive arthritis, erythema nodosum, conjunctivitis, sepsis,&#x000a0;pharyngitis, myocarditis, mesenteric adenitis, and dermatitis. Reactive arthritis is aseptic and asymmetric, and tends to affect the lower limbs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Another feature of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> is erythema nodosum, with lesions appearing 2 to 14 days after the abdominal pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r20">[20]</xref> The lesions are more common in adult females and usually resolve on their own.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Sepsis has been described in infants, older adults, and patients who are immunocompromised or in an iron overload state, with an overall 50% fatality rate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Stool samples should be collected for culture and sensitivity testing within the first 14 days of illness onset. A positive stool culture is confirmatory for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic> infection. However, many laboratories do not routinely test for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic>, so its isolation should be specifically requested.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Cefsulodin-irgasan-novobiocin agar is the most commonly used growth medium to culture&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;However, false-negative stool cultures for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> occur frequently; therefore, a negative culture result cannot rule out&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Other studies depend on the organ system involved. Blood cultures should be obtained from patients with fevers, and throat cultures from patients with pharyngitis. Imaging studies, such as ultrasound, computed tomography, or magnetic resonance imaging, are often required to determine&#x000a0;whether the patient has appendicitis or pseudoappendicitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Serologic tests, including enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays and immunoblotting, are used in Japan and Europe but are less common in the United States. These tests detect IgG, IgA, and IgM. Polymerase chain reaction and immunofluorescence assays have been developed but not widely used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Colonoscopy is not&#x000a0;typically&#x000a0;performed, but when performed, it may reveal aphthoid lesions and ulcers in the terminal ileum. In most cases, the right side of the bowel is involved.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The primary treatment for acute yersiniosis is supportive care with hydration and nutritional support.&#x000a0;Children may need admission for dehydration or sepsis. Most patients experience anorexia and may require an overnight admission for intravenous hydration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Most cases of yersiniosis are self-limited, and antibiotic treatment does not alter or shorten the course in healthy young children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;However, antibiotics may be considered in severe or prolonged disease, in immunocompromised patients, in patients with risk factors for severe <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> infection, such as iron overload, or in older adults.&#x000a0;The antibiotics of choice are quinolones, aminoglycosides, or tetracyclines, in combination with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Tetracyclines should be avoided&#x000a0;during pregnancy and in children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>In some cases, surgery is required to drain an abdominal abscess, and surgical exploration may be warranted if appendicitis cannot be ruled out.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref> Notably, in many cases, pseudoappendicitis and appendicitis cannot be differentiated on a clinical examination or even with imaging. Thus, some patients undergo surgery for the removal of an appendix.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Antimotility agents should be avoided in patients with diarrhea, as they may worsen the infection.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Diagnosis depends on a detailed history, thorough physical examination, and supportive laboratory and radiological findings. Conditions that can present with similar symptoms include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Other causes of infectious diarrhea (secondary viral, bacterial, protozoal, or fungal organisms)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammatory bowel disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diverticulitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Appendicitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Medication-associated colitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ischemic Colitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infectious diseases such as HIV, influenza, dengue, or malaria (particularly in developing countries)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Radiation colitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diversion colitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cancer of the colon or the small bowel</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Yersiniosis generally&#x000a0;has a favorable outcome. Among 2085 laboratory-confirmed cases of <italic toggle="yes">Yersina enterocolitica</italic> detected by&#x000a0;the United States Foodborne Diseases Active Surveillance Network (FoodNet) from 1996 through 2009, only&#x000a0;19 deaths were detected (0.9%), of which 79% occurred in adults aged 60 or older.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;In another study from Norway, only 2 deaths were reported from 458 patients diagnosed with yersiniosis (0.4%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>However, <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> does have other morbidities that include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Sepsis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Liver or spleen abscess</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pseudoappendicitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Erythema nodosum</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pharyngitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mesenteric adenitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Glomerulonephritis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Myocarditis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dermatitis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Conditions that cause hemolysis and release iron increase the risk of systemic infection. In addition, the use of deferoxamine also elevates&#x000a0;the risk of <italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications related to yersiniosis include:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Gastrointestinal:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Bowel perforation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Peritonitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Diffuse ulcerating ileitis and colitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Intussusception</p></list-item><list-item><p>Paralytic ileus</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cholangitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mesenteric vein thrombosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Toxic megacolon</p></list-item><list-item><p>Small bowel necrosis</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Extraintestinal:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Joint pain</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hepatic abscess</p></list-item><list-item><p>Splenic abscess</p></list-item><list-item><p>Septicemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Renal abscess</p></list-item><list-item><p>Osteomyelitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lung abscess</p></list-item><list-item><p>Endocarditis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Suppurative lymphadenitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Skin infection</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mycotic aneurysm</p></list-item><list-item><p>Foreign body infections (catheters/prosthesis)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Myocarditis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Glomerulonephritis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Liver failure</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Patients&#x000a0;presenting with signs and symptoms consistent with appendicitis&#x000a0;should be evaluated by General Surgery to determine whether surgical exploration is warranted.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients with uncomplicated diarrhea should be counseled regarding hydration and electrolyte intake&#x000a0;and observed for signs of decompensation.</p>
        <p>Patients and their caregivers should be counseled to seek care&#x000a0;immediately if the patient develops severe symptoms/complications, such as fever, bloody bowel movements, abdominal distension, toxic appearance, decreased urinary output, among others.</p>
        <p>Patients and the general public should be educated on&#x000a0;hand hygiene, safe food preparation and handling, and avoidance of raw&#x000a0;pork products to prevent infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>At present, there is no vaccine against&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;enterocolitica</italic>, although research is ongoing to identify novel vaccine candidates against this bacterium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31453.r31">[31]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Key factors that should be kept in mind when managing&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic>&#x000a0;infections include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Yersiniosis is an acute diarrheal illness that is caused by 2 species&#x02014;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica&#x000a0;</italic>and<italic toggle="yes"> Y pseudotuberculosis.</italic></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes">Y pestis</italic> is clinically distinct from&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic>.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diagnosis is predominantly based on positive stool culture, but negative culture results do not rule out <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic> infection.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Initial treatment for uncomplicated diarrhea involves hydration, nutrition, and electrolyte replacement.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Emergency care should be sought if the patient develops worsening symptoms or&#x000a0;complications such as bloody stools, high fever, or signs of extraintestinal infection.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Proper hand hygiene, safe food preparation, and avoidance of raw pork products are the best ways to prevent this infection.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31453.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Y enterocolitica</italic> causes a zoonotic infection that may present with acute diarrhea, abdominal pain, mesenteric adenitis, terminal ileitis, or pseudoappendicitis. The pathogen uses multiple virulence factors to invade lymphoid tissue and may trigger complications such as reactive arthritis, erythema nodosum, and sepsis in high-risk individuals. Transmission occurs through contaminated pork, untreated water, household animals, and transfused blood products. Diagnosis relies on stool cultures, imaging, and selective use of serologic or molecular tests. Management centers on hydration, antimicrobial therapy for high-risk patients, and preventive measures that reduce exposure and transmission.</p>
        <p>Effective care for suspected or confirmed yersiniosis requires coordinated assessment, timely diagnostics, and clear communication among all involved healthcare professionals. Primary care providers&#x000a0;and advanced practitioners must recognize clinical patterns that mimic appendicitis, determine appropriate testing, and guide evidence-based treatment decisions. Nurses contribute through symptom monitoring, hydration management, infection control practices, and patient education. Pharmacists support appropriate antimicrobial selection and review safety considerations. Public health professionals, laboratory teams, and surgical staff collaborate to ensure accurate diagnosis, prevent unnecessary surgery, and promptly address complications. Interprofessional coordination strengthens patient safety, enhances outcomes, and ensures delivery of consistent, patient-centered care.</p>
      </sec>
